# The drinker paradox (Smullyan): exists x, (D x -> forall y, D y),
# classically valid, intuitionistically not. Dialectically: the proponent
# claims it, offers the witness c (owing D c -> forall y, D y), and for that
# implication, given D c and an arbitrary y, offers y as a second witness
# to the original claim (owed: its refutation, the claim's continuation),
# throwing D y back. Every owed statement is closed, but the throw of D y
# to its own continuation happens under a second continuation binder (the
# goal forall y, D y): not linear, refused at the pure level; and it cannot
# be split off, since D y mentions the bound y.
lk.
declare Pub : type.
declare c : Pub.
declare Dr : Pub -> bool.
argument p1 : (exists x:Pub, Dr [x] -> (forall y:Pub, Dr [y])).
dixi.
argument p2 : (exists x:Pub, Dr [x] -> (forall y:Pub, Dr [y])).
elim [c].
dixi.
argument p3 : (Dr [c] -> (forall y:Pub, Dr [y])).
elim h.
elim y.
cut (exists x:Pub, Dr [x] -> (forall y:Pub, Dr [y])) k.
elim [y].
elim d.
cut (Dr [y]) m.
axiom d.
axiom k.
next.
dixi.
